---
import QualityLayout from "../../../layouts/QualityLayout.astro";
---

<QualityLayout
  title="Feedback and review — Counterfact quality study"
  description="How to review, question, or correct the Counterfact quality study and its public evidence."
  canonicalPath="/quality/2026/feedback"
  active="feedback"
>
  <article class="appendix-page quality-reading quality-prose">
    <header class="appendix-header">
      <p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/quality/2026">Article</a> / Feedback and review</p>
      <h1>Feedback and review</h1>
      <p>
        This report was generated by Codex and designed to make it easy for a
        human reviewer to trace its claims to public evidence. It invites
        questions, corrections, alternative interpretations, and improvements
        to the study itself.
      </p>
    </header>

    <h2>How to respond</h2>
    <p>
      Email <a href="mailto:pmcelhaney@gmail.com">pmcelhaney@gmail.com</a> for
      feedback, questions, or corrections. Because the source lives in the
      Counterfact repository, readers may also open a
      <a href="https://github.com/counterfact/api-simulator/issues/new">GitHub issue</a>{" "}
      or submit a
      <a href="https://github.com/counterfact/api-simulator/compare">pull request</a>{" "}
      with a proposed correction.
    </p>

    <h2>What to review</h2>
    <p>
      The report separates its claims from the underlying record so that a
      reviewer can inspect the evidence directly. Useful starting points are the
      <a href="/quality/2026/candidates">candidate disposition ledger</a>, the
      <a href="/quality/2026/cases">defect case appendix</a>, the
      <a href="/quality/2026/methodology">methods and reproducibility appendix</a>,
      and the <a href="/quality/2026/data.json">machine-readable dataset</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Review standard</h2>
    <p>
      A helpful review identifies the claim in question and links it to the
      relevant public source, such as an issue, pull request, commit, release,
      or published coverage result. If the evidence supports a different
      interpretation, the report should be corrected rather than defended.
    </p>
  </article>
</QualityLayout>
